1
00:00:00,350 --> 00:00:05,410
You've built twelve real artifacts across this course: a PRD template, a golden set,

2
00:00:05,410 --> 00:00:09,325
a rubric, a cost model, a risk register, a metric tree, and more.

3
00:00:09,325 --> 00:00:14,023
Dumped into a folder and linked in an email, they prove you attended a course.

4
00:00:14,023 --> 00:00:19,022
Selected and sequenced around one real feature, they prove something an interviewer

5
00:00:19,022 --> 00:00:24,082
actually can't get from a resume line: that you know what order the thinking happens

6
00:00:24,082 --> 00:00:24,263
in.

7
00:00:24,263 --> 00:00:28,118
Most PM portfolios show a shipped feature and a nice screenshot.

8
00:00:28,118 --> 00:00:32,997
Almost none show the eval rubric, the cost model, or the risk register behind it,

9
00:00:32,997 --> 00:00:35,526
because most PMs never formally built one.

10
00:00:35,526 --> 00:00:40,285
That gap is exactly where this course's artifacts stop being homework and start

11
00:00:40,285 --> 00:00:42,152
being a genuine differentiator.

12
00:00:42,152 --> 00:00:46,368
This isn't about hiding the other eight behind the four you lead with.

13
00:00:46,368 --> 00:00:51,247
All twelve stay available, linked or attached, for anyone who wants to go deeper.

14
00:00:51,247 --> 00:00:56,066
The four you actually walk through out loud, in the room, are the ones doing the

15
00:00:56,066 --> 00:00:56,909
work of proof.

16
00:00:56,909 --> 00:01:01,909
The rest are there for the person who asks a genuinely specific follow-up and wants

17
00:01:01,909 --> 00:01:02,631
to see more.

18
00:01:03,181 --> 00:01:07,847
Pick one feature, real or from this course's exercises, and walk it through these

19
00:01:07,847 --> 00:01:12,167
four questions in this exact order, because it's the order a genuinely good

20
00:01:12,167 --> 00:01:16,660
interviewer already asks them in, whether or not they've ever seen this table.

21
00:01:16,660 --> 00:01:21,441
If you're choosing between a real feature from your own work and one built entirely

22
00:01:21,441 --> 00:01:26,106
from this course's exercises, the real one usually wins, even if it's smaller and

23
00:01:26,106 --> 00:01:26,913
less polished.

24
00:01:26,913 --> 00:01:31,578
Genuine judgement under real constraints reads differently from a clean exercise,

25
00:01:31,578 --> 00:01:35,553
and most interviewers can tell the difference within a few questions.

26
00:01:35,553 --> 00:01:40,391
Resist the instinct to show all twelve artifacts because more feels like more proof.

27
00:01:40,391 --> 00:01:41,428
It's the opposite.

28
00:01:41,428 --> 00:01:45,345
Twelve artifacts with no throughline reads as a checklist completed.

29
00:01:45,345 --> 00:01:49,665
Four artifacts walking one feature from spec to shippable reads as a way of

30
00:01:49,665 --> 00:01:53,351
thinking, which is the actual thing being evaluated in the room.

31
00:01:53,901 --> 00:01:57,067
Why leave two strong artifacts out of the main four?

32
00:01:57,067 --> 00:02:02,121
Because a portfolio walkthrough that tries to cover every section of this course in

33
00:02:02,121 --> 00:02:06,687
one sitting stops being a story and becomes a syllabus recited from memory.

34
00:02:06,687 --> 00:02:11,071
Four questions, answered well, beats twelve topics mentioned in passing.

35
00:02:11,071 --> 00:02:16,064
Notice, too, that the four-question sequence works because each answer sets up the

36
00:02:16,064 --> 00:02:17,525
next question naturally.

37
00:02:17,525 --> 00:02:22,578
Once the PRD's threshold is on the table, the golden set's pass rate is the obvious

38
00:02:22,578 --> 00:02:24,527
next thing anyone would ask for.

39
00:02:24,527 --> 00:02:29,397
Once the pass rate is real, the cost number is what makes it a business decision

40
00:02:29,397 --> 00:02:30,493
instead of a demo.

41
00:02:30,493 --> 00:02:32,076
The order isn't arbitrary.

42
00:02:32,076 --> 00:02:35,303
It's the sequence a good question naturally produces.

43
00:02:35,853 --> 00:02:40,941
Say the honest caveat plainly, because this is the exact doctrine this whole course

44
00:02:40,941 --> 00:02:45,599
has run on, aimed one last time at you instead of a feature you're building.

45
00:02:45,599 --> 00:02:50,564
"I practised this discipline on a hypothetical feature to make sure I could do it

46
00:02:50,564 --> 00:02:54,180
for real" is a completely legitimate, respectable sentence.

47
00:02:54,180 --> 00:02:58,961
Letting an artifact's polish imply it came from a real shipped product when it

48
00:02:58,961 --> 00:03:03,681
didn't is the one move that turns a genuine portfolio into something a single

49
00:03:03,681 --> 00:03:06,071
follow-up question can quietly unravel.

50
00:03:06,071 --> 00:03:08,768
The honest version survives every follow-up.

51
00:03:08,768 --> 00:03:10,974
The other kind survives exactly one.

52
00:03:10,974 --> 00:03:15,755
This matters more than it might seem worth a whole caveat, because the cost of

53
00:03:15,755 --> 00:03:18,943
getting caught is specific and hard to recover from.

54
00:03:18,943 --> 00:03:23,785
An interviewer who catches one inflated claim doesn't just discount that claim.

55
00:03:23,785 --> 00:03:28,382
They quietly re-evaluate everything else you said in the same conversation,

56
00:03:28,382 --> 00:03:31,201
including the parts that were completely true.

57
00:03:31,751 --> 00:03:36,548
That's the standard worth building the rest of the portfolio to, and the exercise is

58
00:03:36,548 --> 00:03:40,146
worth doing today, on the artifact you're actually proudest of.

59
00:03:40,146 --> 00:03:44,828
Most people find the two sentences harder to write than the artifact was to build,

60
00:03:44,828 --> 00:03:49,511
because naming the exact question it answers, precisely, is a different skill from

61
00:03:49,511 --> 00:03:52,537
having built something that answers it instinctively.

62
00:03:52,537 --> 00:03:57,334
That's worth practising before an interview room is the first place you're doing it.

63
00:03:57,334 --> 00:04:01,731
Do this for more than one artifact if you have time, not just your favourite.

64
00:04:01,731 --> 00:04:06,299
The exercise gets easier by the third attempt, and the version of you doing it a

65
00:04:06,299 --> 00:04:11,096
fourth time is the version actually ready for a real interview room, not the version

66
00:04:11,096 --> 00:04:15,436
who wrote two good sentences once and hoped the rest would follow naturally.

67
00:04:15,986 --> 00:04:17,776
One thing before you move on.

68
00:04:17,776 --> 00:04:19,874
Pick the four and the order first.

69
00:04:19,874 --> 00:04:25,057
Use this lecture's table as the starting sequence, and adjust it only if your actual

70
00:04:25,057 --> 00:04:29,130
feature's story genuinely asks its questions in a different order.

71
00:04:29,130 --> 00:04:33,820
Then label every artifact honestly, in writing, before anyone else reads it.

72
00:04:33,820 --> 00:04:38,510
That label is what turns a portfolio into something you can defend under any

73
00:04:38,510 --> 00:04:41,102
question, not just the ones you rehearsed.

74
00:04:41,102 --> 00:04:44,064
Neither of these requires building anything new.

75
00:04:44,064 --> 00:04:45,915
Every artifact already exists.

76
00:04:45,915 --> 00:04:50,914
This is entirely about selection and honesty, the cheapest, highest-leverage work

77
00:04:50,914 --> 00:04:53,382
available to you in this entire section.

78
00:04:53,382 --> 00:04:58,196
Next lecture is interview questions you can now answer, the specific ones this

79
00:04:58,196 --> 00:05:00,417
course was built to prepare you for.
